<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kafue River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kafue is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, about 960 km long and the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> lying wholly within the country. It rises near the Congo border, flows past the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copperbelt">Copperbelt</a>, crosses the wide Kafue Flats and joins the Zambezi downstream of Kariba. In the early colonial period it roughly divided North-Western from North-Eastern Rhodesia, and its dams now supply much of Zambia's hydroelectric power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kafue-river.jpg" alt="Kafue River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paulmaz at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kafue_river02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Kafue rises at an elevation of 1,350 metres on a flat plateau of miombo woodland on the Congo–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> watershed, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>'s North-western Province, 120 km north-west of Chingola. It begins as a trickle from marshy dambos and meanders sluggishly south-east. Past the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copperbelt">Copperbelt</a> towns of Chililabombwe, Chingola and Mufulira it turns south-west at Kitwe and then forms a maze of channels and lagoons on a swampy floodplain beside the Lukanga Swamp, where the combined wetland in the rains exceeds 6,000 square kilometres.</p><p>It then enters Kafue National Park, the second largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and skirts the Busanga Plain, known for herds of buffalo, zebra and antelope. At the Itezhi-Tezhi Gap it turns east through a ridge about 100 metres high. Beyond lie the Kafue Flats, a floodplain about 240 km long and 50 km wide, home to tens of thousands of Kafue lechwe and many birds and formerly called Butwa after the Twa people. Below Kafue town the river drops 550 metres over 60 km in the Kafue Gorge and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> about 20 km north of Chirundu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river once flowed south all the way to Lake Makgadikgadi and on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, like the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>, Okavango and Cuando. Uplift of the land and a rift valley changed this, and the Kafue cut the Itezhi-Tezhi Gap and turned east. The lower Zambezi, originally a separate river, cut back through the rift valleys and eventually captured it.</p><p>The Kafue Railway Bridge, opened in 1906 to connect Kafue with Mazabuka, was meant to be part of the Cape to Cairo Railway. The first road bridge was built across the Thames in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, dismantled afterwards and installed about 9 km downstream of the railway bridge; before that, road traffic crossed on a pontoon. The Itezhi-Tezhi Dam, built in 1977, forms a reservoir 50 km long, and a second dam at Kafue Gorge generates electricity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is used for irrigation and hydroelectric power and is the most central and urban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>'s principal rivers: more than half of the country's people live in its basin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar plantations</a> around Mazabuka draw on it, notably the Nakambala Sugar Estate. Lochinvar and Blue Lagoon National Parks lie on the Kafue Flats, and the Hippo Pool near Chingola is a national monument.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kafue River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Company agents and British officials extended control over the lands east of the <b>Kafue</b> in the 1890s, defeating Mpezeni's Ngoni Kingdom in 1898 and bringing the Bemba Kingdom under control in 1898-1899.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-eastern-rhodesia">North-Eastern Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
